msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:393
#: e71880e63ffa4e229c13cccec1868690
msgid "Using template-based Windows AppVMs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:396
#: 894188b1c06f43e483596d5fc3790d6d
msgid ""
"Qubes allows HVM VMs to share a common root filesystem from a select "
"Template VM, just as for Linux AppVMs. This mode is not limited to Windows "
"AppVMs, and can be used for any HVM (e.g. FreeBSD running in a HVM)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:398
#: 5637d0913ae942eb91c1a4d3a3860354
msgid ""
"In order to create an HVM TemplateVM, the type “TemplateVM” has to be "
"selected on creating the VM. Then set memory as appropriate, and install the "
"Windows OS (or any other OS) into this template the same way as you would "
"install it into a normal HVM – please see instructions on :doc:`this page </"
"user/advanced-topics/standalones-and-hvms>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:400
#: a32c08bc061c4f74adb44a64a6cba9f1
msgid ""
"If you use this Template as it is, then any HVMs that use it will "
"effectively be DisposableVMs - the User directory will be wiped when the HVM "
"is closed down."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:402
#: 330a710215ed4e2ba681f73b8ca41ece
msgid ""
"If you want to retain the User directory between reboots, then it would make "
"sense to store the ``C:\\Users`` directory on the 2nd disk, which is "
"automatically exposed by Qubes to all HVMs. This 2nd disk is backed by the "
"``private.img`` file in the AppVMs and is not reset upon AppVMs reboot, so "
"the user’s directories and profiles would survive the AppVMs reboot, unlike "
"the “root” filesystem, which will be reverted to the “golden image” from the "
"Template VM automatically. To facilitate such separation of user profiles, "
"Qubes Windows Tools provide an option to automatically move the ``C:"
"\\Users`` directory to the 2nd disk backed by ``private.img``. It’s a "
"selectable feature of the installer. For Windows 7, the private disk must be "
"renamed to ``Q:`` before QWT installation (see above); for Windows 10 and "
"11, this renaming occurs automatically during QWT installation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:404
#: 8dc6ffdeb2514e0bae154eedcc06dfe5
msgid ""
"If that feature is selected during installation, completion of the process "
"requires two reboots:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:406
#: bba0d8e4ab9d431cb165bcdc3efa2883
msgid ""
"The private disk is initialised and formatted on the first reboot after "
"tools installation. It can’t be done **during** the installation because Xen "
"mass storage drivers are not yet active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:408
#: b4428080367b4959af03880956be035b
msgid ""
"User profiles are moved to the private disk on the next reboot after the "
"private disk is initialised. A reboot is required because the “mover "
"utility” runs very early in the boot process so OS can’t yet lock any files "
"in there. This can take some time depending on the profiles’ size, and "
"because the GUI agent is not yet active, dom0/Qubes Manager may complain "
"that the AppVM failed to boot. That’s a false alarm (you can increase the "
"AppVM’s default boot timeout using ``qvm-prefs``), the VM should appear "
"“green” in Qubes Manager shortly after."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../user/templates/windows/qubes-windows-tools.rst:411
#: 51cef94feb5645eba4ea88e44c06645e
msgid ""
"It also makes sense to disable Automatic Updates for all the template-based "
"AppVMs – of course, this should be done in the Template VM, not in "
"individual AppVMs, because the system-wide settings are stored in the root "
"filesystem (which holds the system-wide registry hives). Then, periodically "
"check for updates in the Template VM, and the changes will be carried over "
"to any child AppVMs."
msgstr ""

